${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -Sensitized Upconversion Metal-Organic Frameworks for Mitochondria-Targeted Amplified Photodynamic Therapy
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by coordination-driven assembly of metal ions and organic linkers, have shown great potential in nanomedicine applications.[1] In particular, porphyrinic nanoscale MOFs (nMOFs) have recently emerged as a promising nanophotosensitizers (nPSs) for photodynamic therapy (PDT).[2] The self-assembled synthetic mechanism of nMOFs allows for high loading content of porphyrinic PSs, wherein the self-quenching from aggregation can be prevented. The porous structures of nMOFs enable improved PDT efficacy over other nPSs based on the enhanced diffusion of reactive oxygen species (ROS).[2] However, the in vivo applications of these nMOF-based PDT systems are limited by the shallow tissue penetration of ultraviolet (UV)/visible light that is required to excite the porphyrins. In addition, these systems suffer from the lack of spatial control over the photodynamic effect and, therefore, a limited therapeutic efficiency. To tackle these issues, the construction of near-infrared (NIR) light-activated nMOFs combined with precise spatial control is highly desirable for the development of next generations of PDT system.
Mitochondria, the cells' powerhouse and suicidal weapon store, regulates a myriad of biological processes ranging from biosynthesis and signaling to apoptosis.[3] Increasing evidence suggests that mitochondrial homeostasis plays critical roles in cancer biology, such as  cancer initiation, growth, and metastasis.[4] Recently, mitochondria is emerging as a potential therapeutic target for cancer.[4b-e] In particular, regulation of the levels of ROS in mitochondria could activate the cell death machinery and therefore is a promising approach for enhanced cancer therapy.[4c] Nevertheless, development of effective strategies for modulation of mitochondrial ROS homeostasis with high efficiency remains a challenge.
As optical nanotransducers, lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs) can emit high-energy UV or visible light upon the excitation with the low-energy NIR light (typically ca. 980 nm),[5] which showing increasing promise for NIR-mediated photoregulation applications, including manipulation of biosensing,[6] neural activity,[7] and biomedicine.[8] Inspired by these achievements, we designed a ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized upconversion MOFs (designated as UCMTs) for NIR lighttriggered, mitochondria-targeted PDT (Scheme 1). The UCMTs possess a Janus nanostructure made of ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized UCNPs and porphyrinic nMOFs with further surface functionalization of triphenylphosphine (TPP), a mitochondria-targeted ligand. The UCMTs allow for the 808 NIR light-activated ROS production by the efficient resonance energy transfer from ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized UCNP to MOF domain. Notably, the use of $808{\mathsf{n m}}$ light (achieved through ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized upconversion process) could effectively avoid the laser irradiation-induced heating effect because of minimum absorption of biological tissue at this NIR wavelength.
Moreover, the NIR light triggered in situ generation of ROS in mitochondria could cause depolarization of the mitochondrial membrane and the cytosolic release of pro-apoptotic proteins, leading to amplified therapeutic efficacy.
The schematic illustration for the structure of UCMTs was presented in Figure 1a. UCMTs were prepared by a three-step approach as follows: synthesis of two-shelled UCNPs, anisotropic growth of porphyrinic nMOFs on their surface, and further surface modification of TPP. The ${\mathsf{N a G d F}}_{4}$ :Yb,Er core with an average diameter of ${\sim}20\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S1) was first fabricated by a modified thermal decomposition method. To achieve efficient upconversion luminescence (UCL) under $808\mathsf{n m}$ light excitation, $\mathsf{N d}^{3+}/\mathsf{Y}\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ coincorporated shell $(\mathsf{N a G d F}_{4};\mathsf{N d},\mathsf{Y b})$ was grown onto their surface via an epitaxial growth method $(\sim26\ \mathsf{n m})$ . Then, a pure $N a G d F_{4}$ shell was coated on the NPs to improve the UCL efficacy by preventing the surface-related quenching effect. The resulted core-shell-shell UCNPs had a mean diameter of ${\sim}32$ nm (Figure 1b). The UCNPs in high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images exposed (110) facets (Figure S2).
Next, the UCNPs were modified with polyvinylpyrolidone (PVP) for selective growth of $Z r$ -based MOFs on their surface (to yield UCMs). At last, TPP molecules were modified onto the surface of Janus UCMs through coordination interaction between the carboxylate group of TPP and coordinatively unsaturated metal sites in $Z\mathsf{r}_{6}$ clusters on the surface of MOF domain.[2e,9] Figure 1c and d presented the TEM images of UCMTs. nMOFs was found to grow anisotropically on one side of the UCNP, forming Janus nanostructures. The size distribution of the UCNP, nMOF and UCMTs was $32{\pm}2$ , $57\pm9$ , and $76\pm10\mathsf{n m}$ , respectively (Figure S3). More detailed composition information of Janus UCMTs was investigated by EDS elemental mapping images (Figure 1e) and line scan (Figure S4) obtained from HAADF-STEM. Consistent with the TEM observation, Nd and Gd elements are distributed on one side, while Zr and N elements are mainly distributed on the other side. The XRD pattern in Figure S5 illustrated diffraction peaks of cubic crystalline PCN-224 MOFs and hexagonal phase of NaGdF4. We found that no Janus nanostructure was formed when the UCNPs without PVP-coating were used for the synthesis (Figure S6), indicating that PVP plays a critical role for heterogeneous growth of the Janus structure. Statistic analysis with TEM revealed that the Janus structures was obtained in $\sim81\%$ yield. In addition, UCMTs possess a good stability as they remain intact in water and HEPES buffer for one day (Figure S7).
The $N d^{3+}N b^{3+}/E r^{3+}$ cascade sensitized core-shell-shell UCNPs with a constitutional excitation at 808 nm are utilized here as energy donors for PDT. As presented in Figure 2a and b, the primary sensitizers of ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ absorb photons at $^{808\mathsf{n m}}$ , while the $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ ions act as bridging ions for the energy transfer from the ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ ions to the emitters of $\mathsf{E r}^{3+}$ . The UCL spectra of $\mathsf{N a G d F}_{4}{\cdot}\mathsf{Y b,E r@N a G d F}_{4}{\cdot}\mathsf{Y b,N d}$ (labeled as E $r@{\mathsf{N d}})$ showed the typical emission peaks of $\mathsf{E r}^{3+}$ at 521 nm, 540 and $660~\mathsf{n m}$ under the $^{808\ \mathsf{n m}}$ NIR laser excitation (Figure 2c), which could be assigned to the transitions from $^2{\mathsf{H}}_{11/2}$ to $^4|_{15/2}$ , ${^4}\mathsf{S}_{3/2}$ to $^4|_{15/2}$ , and ${^4}\mathsf{F}_{9/2}$ to $^4|_{15/2}$ , respectively. Further growth of an epitaxial shell of $N a G d F_{4}$ on $E r@{\mathsf{N d}}$ $(\mathsf{E r@N d@G d})$ can obviously enhance the UCL emission intensity $(\sim4.9$ times) due to the suppression of surface-related deactivations (Figure 2c).[8] In the UV−vis absorption spectrum of UCMTs, characteristic $\mathsf{Q}$ -bands of porphyrins at 520, 554, 590, and $645~\mathsf{n m}$ were observed, which has a good overlap with the UCL spectrum of UCNPs. The spectra overlap along with the close distance between the two domains in Janus UCMTs could facilitate the luminescence resonance energy transfer (LRET) from UCNP side to MOFs domain. Both the emission intensity (Figure 2e) and the lifetime of UCL (Figure S8) decreased significantly after growth of MOFs on the UCNPs, indicating the effective LRET within the Janus nanostructure. To evaluate the photodynamic property, $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ indicator (SOSG) was used. Upon $^{808\ \mathsf{n m}}$ laser irradiation, a significant increase of the fluorescence of SOSG at $528\ \mathsf{n m}$ was observed for UCMTs (Figure 2f). In contrast, UCMTs without NIR irradiation showed no obvious fluorescence change. As a control, the mixture solution of MOF NPs and UCNPs was unable to generate $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ under the $808\ \mathsf{n m}\ \mathsf{N I R}$ irradiation (Figure S9), confirming the importance of Janus structure for the NIR lighttriggered $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation. Furthermore, characteristic $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ -induced signals were observed in the electron spin resonance (ESR) spectrum of UCMTs under NIR irradiation (Figure S10). These data indicated that the effective energy transfer within UCMTs enables the activation of MOFs to produce ROS under $808~\mathsf{n m}$ NIR light irradiation.
To verify the mitochondria-targeting capability of UCMTs, colocalization experiments were performed in 4T1 cells with UCMs as control. As shown in Figure 3a, the fluorescence of MOFs in the cells incubated with UCMTs showed good overlaps with the signals of Mito-Tracker Red. In contrast, UCMs colocalize poorly with Mito-Tracker Red. These results validated that TPP ligands could effectively drive the accumulation of UCMTs into mitochondria. To investigate intracellualr $^1{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ generation by UCMTs, dichlorofluorescein diacetate (DCF-DA) was used as a indicator. As shown in Figure 3b and Figure S11, the cells treated with UCMTs and 808 nm laser irradiation showed bright DCF fluorescence, while no obvious green fluorescence was seen for the cells treated with nonirradiated UCMTs or irradiated MOFTs (TPP-modified MOF NPs). The quantitative analysis showed that the intracellular fluorescence intensity of DCF for the cells treated with UCMTs $^+$ NIR was 3.7-fold higher than that of cells incubated with UCMTs without NIR irradiation (Figure S12).
The photodynamic cytotoxicity of UCMTs was then evaluated through a standard cell viability assay (CKK-8 assay). As shown in Figure 3c, without photoirradiation, there was negligible decrease in the viability of the UCMTs-treated 4T1 cells. Upon the $808~\mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation, the cell viability of UCMTs-treated cells decreased to $27.0\%$ , 1.9-fold lower than that of UCMs. In addition, the concentration-dependent photodynamic cytotoxicity of UCMTs was observed (Figure S13). As a control, no appreciable cell killing effect was noticed for cells incubated with UCNPs or MOFTs followed by NIR light irradiation. Together, these results indicated that the mitochondria-targeting feature of UCMTs allows for the in situ generation of ROS in mitochondria to enhance the PDT efficacy.
To investigate the mechanism of the targeting-mediated PDT enhancement, the fluorescent probes JC-1 was used as an indicator to examine the change of mitochondrial membrane potential (MMP). As shown in Figure 3d, NIR irradiation of intracellular UCMTs increased the ratio of green-to-red signal, indicating depolarized MMP. The quantitation showed that the cellular ratio of green-to-red signal elicited by UCMTs $^+$ NIR was 16.8-fold higher than that induced by nonirradiated UCMTs (Figure S14). Furthermore, cytosolic release of cytochrome c, a pro-apoptotic protein, was observed for the cells treated with UCMTs and NIR irradiation (Figure S15). Since it is well known that the release of cytochrome c can initiate the apoptotic cascade and lead to the activation of caspase-3, we then evaluated the activity of caspase-3 by using FITC-conjugated cleaved caspase3 antibody. As expect, the expression of caspase-3 increased significantly in the UCMTs $+\mathsf{N I R}$ group (Figure 3e). In contrast, control studies using UCMs with NIR irradiation exerted much less effect on the MMP decreasing, cytochrome c release, and caspase-3 activation (Figure S16). Furthermore, we found an upregulated expression in mitochondria-mediated apoptotic genes (BAD, BAX, BAK and NOXA) in the cells treated with UCMTs $^+$ NIR compared to control UCMTs and $\mathsf{U C M s}+\mathsf{N I R}$ groups (Figure S17), confirming the mitochondria-mediated mechanism of apoptosis. Taken together, UCMTs allows to 808 NIR light triggered release of $^10_{2}$ into mitochondria and causes the depolarization of MMP, leading to the release of mitochondrial apoptotic factors into the cytosol, activation of caspase-3 and, eventually, amplified cell apoptosis.
Current UCNP-based PDT systems often use $980\ \mathsf{n m\ N I R}$ light as the excitation source, which has a high risk of tissue damage due to its heating effect.[8b,e] Such overheating issue could be alleviated by lowering the laser power with long-time irradiation or the use of high power with an interval between every short-time irradiation.[8b] In our design, we constructed ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}.$ - sensitized UCMTs to shift their irradiation wavelength to a shorter wavelength of $808~\mathsf{n m}$ , while getting into the biological window $(700-900~\mathsf{n m})$ . To investigate NIR-induced heating effect on tissues, real-time temperature of the mice skin was measured upon irradiation with $980\mathsf{n m}$ and 808 nm laser of different power. The temperature in the irradiated area showed no obvious change after 10 min irradiation with $808\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser $(0.3\ \mathsf{W}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , the maximum permissible exposure of skin), while the temperature increased from 25.0 oC to $31.8\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ upon the 980 nm laser irradiation for 5 min under the same conditions (Figure S18). As the power of laser increases, the difference in temperature rises became larger. The local temperature of tissue increased rapidly up to $51.1\textdegree C$ only after 3 min exposure to the $980\mathsf{n m}$ laser of 1.0 $\mathsf{W}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ , whereas a slight rise in temperature $(5.3\ \circ_{\mathsf{C}})$ was observed after $^{808\ \mathsf{n m}}$ laser irradiation for 10 minutes (Figure 4a,b). The prevention of the tissue damage induced by $980~\mathsf{n m}$ laser irradiation was further confirmed by the analysis of irradiated tissue sections by hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining (Figure S19). These results suggest that $808{\mathsf{n m}}$ laser has a much lower overheating effect than $980~\mathsf{n m}$ laser and could be superior for PDT.
The potential of UCMTs for $808~\mathsf{n m}$ light-triggered amplified cancer therapy was evaluated in vivo. 4T1 tumor-bearing mice were treated with UCMs, UCMTs, or saline, and NIR laser irradiation was applied to mice at $2h$ post-injection. All groups showed no obvious weight loss for 14 days after treatment (Figure S20). Without NIR irradiation, the tumor for UCMs or UCMTs treated mice showed similar growth rate as that for saline-treated group (Figure 4c), suggesting no therapeutic effect for both NPs. With NIR irradiation, the tumor growth of UCMs-treated mice was delayed; in contrast, the tratment with UCMTs $^+$ NIR exhibited stronger inhibition on tumor growth during the 14-day period of observation, indicating that the introduction of mitochondriatargeting capability enhanced PDT efficacy. This superior antitumor effect was also verified by the representative photographs (Figure 4d) and average weights (Figure S21) of the final tumors excised from mice of each group. The therapeutic effect was further evaluated by H&E staining and terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase biotin-dUTP nick-end labeling (TUNEL) assay (Figure 4e). The H&E staining showed more obvious cellular necrosis and apoptosis for UCMTs-treated group relative to UCMs-treated mice with NIR irradiation. The TUNEL assay also revealed that the treatment with UCMTs $^+$ NIR led to more apoptotic cells than other groups.
In summary, a NIR light-activated PDT nanoplatform was designed and synthesized based on the combination of porphyrinic MOFs and UCNPs with mitochondria-targeting strategy. By virtue of the $N{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized upconversion process and efficient energy transfer from UCNP to MOF domain in Janus UCMTs, the nanoplatform allows for generation of $^10_{2}$ with 808 nm laser to minimize the laser irradiation-induced overheating effect. More importantly, mitochondria-targeted PDT induced the initiation of intrinsic apoptotic pathway and, thereby, led to superior therapeutic effect over nontargeted therapy. This work demonstrates an effective strategy for MOF-based PDT via the introduction of the ${\mathsf{N d}}^{3+}$ -sensitized upconversion technology and mitochondria-targeted tools.
